  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Eleven runners left on base. Nothing to show for it.",
    "what_this_means": "They never led. Kansas City took the early damage and held on through a day game where the Padres manufactured chances but couldn't convert any of them. Griffin Canning lasted only 3.2 innings, but the bullpen that followed. Waldron, Marinaccio, Brito combining for 4.1 frames. Gave the lineup exactly what it needed: clean innings and time to catch up. The team didn't get there. They went 0-for-11 in runners-in-scoring-position situations, leaving runners on in five separate innings, with the critical damage in the second, fourth, fifth, and sixth. Kansas City scored in the first and sixth. That was enough. This is inconsistency showing its teeth in real time: the pitching set the table, the offense left it bare. They won't survive many games like this one, even when the pen does its job.",
    "what_to_watch": "Noah Cameron takes the mound for Kansas City tonight at 11:10 AM PT, carrying a 4.89 ERA into a matchup where Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez will have the ball for San Diego. This is a chance to right the imbalance that cost them yesterday.",
